From greg at warnes.net  Thu Jul  5 16:07:20 2007
From: greg at warnes.net (Gregory Warnes)
Date: Thu, 05 Jul 2007 12:07:20 -0400
Subject: Re: [Rpy] RPy questions
In-Reply-To: <6bbd27910707022335q1a465801k278cb8da0094ac29@mail.gmail.com>
References: <6bbd27910707022335q1a465801k278cb8da0094ac29@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <75A56C1C-819D-421B-A73F-18A25A6F53CB@warnes.net>

Hi Min-yi,

I'm glad to hear that RPy is useful to you.

Remember that R and Python have different object types, and it is  
often necessary to convert between them.  This conversion often loses  
information.  RPy has a number of "conversion modes" to control how  
this conversion occurs.  The default conversion mode is  
"BASIC_CONVERSION" which attempts to convert every R object into a  
basic python object when it is returned.  So, in the code

> from rpy import *
> a = r.read_table("datafile")

the return value _in_R_ of read.table, and this is getting converted  
into a hash when it is returned to Python.

To avoid this, either change the default mode with:

 >>> set_default_mode(BASIC_CONVERSION)
 >>> a = r.read_table("datafile")

or for just this one call via

 >>> a = with_mode(NO_CONVERSION, r.read_table)("datafile")

-Greg

[Tony: this is a FAQ for rpy]

On Jul 3, 2007, at 2:35AM , Min-yi Shen wrote:

